std::unordered_map<int64_t,size_t> iidx;
std::unordered_map<std::string,size_t> sidx;
int64_t nextIndex = 0;
```
nextIndex semantics: PHP's next auto index = (max int key ever seen)+1... Actually PHP: when adding an element without a key, index = current max int key + 1; if there was no int key, 0. It uses `nNextFreeElement` which is updated as max(intkey)+1 when adding int keys. E.g. `$a[5]=1; $a[]=2;` → 2 at index 6. `$a = [3, 'x'=>1, 4]` → 4 at index 4. Also after unset of last element, next stays. Implement: nextIndex starts 0; when adding int key k, nextIndex = max(nextIndex, k+1). When appending use nextIndex.

